WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.206
Parameterized queries are the central defensive pattern for values supplied to SQL commands.

2
00:00:06.206 --> 00:00:10.996
The command describes the operation the application intends to perform.

3
00:00:10.996 --> 00:00:20.440
Parameters carry the values used by that operation, such as customer IDs, tenant IDs, date ranges, statuses, search terms, or paging values.

4
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:26.174
Keeping those roles separate prevents values from becoming database instruction text.

5
00:00:26.174 --> 00:00:27.523
ADO.

6
00:00:27.523 --> 00:00:33.054
NET and database providers support command and parameter APIs for this separation.

7
00:00:33.054 --> 00:00:45.062
The exact classes and providers can vary, but the design principle remains the same: command text is defined by the application, and variable values are bound through parameters.

8
00:00:45.062 --> 00:00:52.212
Reviewers should be able to see that separation without having to reason about manual string construction.

9
00:00:52.212 --> 00:00:54.033
Type-safe handling matters.

10
00:00:54.033 --> 00:01:02.870
Values such as identifiers, dates, numeric ranges, booleans, statuses, and enum-like options should be represented as appropriate .

11
00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.029
NET types as early as practical.

12
00:01:05.029 --> 00:01:13.326
Stronger types reduce ambiguous conversions, make validation easier, and help data-access code express intent more clearly.

13
00:01:13.326 --> 00:01:20.072
Escaping, blacklisting, and character replacement are not reliable substitutes for parameterization.

14
00:01:20.072 --> 00:01:30.056
They depend on assumptions about database syntax, encoding, provider behavior, future query changes, and every possible place a value might be used.

15
00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:38.488
A safe pattern should not require every developer to predict how a database parser might interpret a manually altered string.

16
00:01:38.488 --> 00:01:45.571
String concatenation and interpolation become risky when variable data is used to build SQL command text.

17
00:01:45.571 --> 00:01:53.868
Even when a value looks harmless, the code pattern is difficult to audit and may be copied into less controlled situations.

18
00:01:53.868 --> 00:02:00.547
Teams should prefer approved query APIs and helper functions that make unsafe construction unusual.

19
00:02:00.547 --> 00:02:02.908
Consistency is part of the defense.

20
00:02:02.908 --> 00:02:15.387
If one repository uses safe parameterized commands, another uses a micro-ORM, and a third builds SQL fragments through custom helpers, reviewers need clear local rules for each pattern.

21
00:02:15.387 --> 00:02:24.224
Approved examples, code review checklists, and shared data-access utilities help make safe query construction the default behavior.
